/*! \mainpage SLiQ-MoCS
* SLiQ-MoCS (Simple Lightweight Monte-Carlo Simulator) aims to provide a direct
* understandable approach to simulating spin systems and their interaction.
* Throughout a variety of classes and their methods, one can run basic
* simulation or extend them easily using the built-in polymorphism of the code.
*
* The later is documented using Doxygen to avoid any misunderstanding in
* code design, and provide the user a simple solution to using the
* program.

\section code_design Code Design
In the aim of running a Quantum Monte-Carlo simulation, several components are
necessary. We choose to separate these in several classes under a similar global
namespace for the project, qmc. These components are:
- A \ref code_mcstep "user interface" to the simulation. We use qmc::MCStep for these purposes.
- A \ref code_ssedata "storage for simulation data"
    + Spin and operator states are managed by qmc::SSEData
    + Observables, needed for measurements, are inherited from qmc::Observable
- A \ref code_lattice "lattice", containing structural information about the system. This task is
  handled by qmc::Lattice and its children.
*/

/*! \page code_mcstep User-Simulation Interface
\brief Details the structural design of qmc::MCStep.

All user interaction with the simulation and its results is managed by
qmc::MCStep. This class allows the _spawning_ of simulation objects, and takes
a qmc::Lattice object instantiated by the user to perform necessary set-up
steps. It manages:
- Random number generators (TODO need refs)
- Simulation data, stored dynamically at the adress qmc::MCStep::sse_data.
- A qmc::Lattice and set of observables (need refs) provided by the user
*/

/*! \page code_ssedata Simulation Data Management
\brief Explains how simulation data is stored, accessed and modified.

The class qmc::SSEData is tasked with handling the spin and operator states of
the system. It provides numerous public methods to access those parameters
selectively. Modification of the class attributes, however, occurs only through
private methods, in order to hide class data effectively. The access to these
methods is however granted to qmc::MCStep for simplicity (through forward
declaration as friend):

    friend class MCStep;

\section code_ssedata_conv Storage Conventions
We define storage conventions used throughout the code. One might encounter such
conventions in methods documentation too.

\subsection code_ssedata_conv_spins Spin Storage Conventions
Spiins
\subsection code_ssedata_conv_oprtr Operator Storage Conventions
Operaaators
\subsection code_ssedata_conv_legs Link vertex storage conventions
Leeegs
*/

